\subsection{Diagnosing What a Skill Has Learned}

Let $s_x$ denote the completed skill for source task $x$, and let $T_k(x)$ denote a task variant. The skill remains fixed when it is used on $T_k(x)$. We compare skill-assisted and no-skill executions of the same task or variant using its verifier. A positive paired difference indicates utility in that setting. Its persistence across changes indicates the observed scope of reuse.

We distinguish three questions. First, do positive source-task gains recur across runs? Second, how does the same skill perform when the representation, instance, or requirements change? Third, which guidance in the skill is associated with successful or failed reuse? The first two questions are answered by paired outcomes. The third is examined through skill contents, execution behavior, and the published construction procedures. The executor can adapt its actions on a variant, but the skill itself is not revised during this evaluation.

