\section{Discussion: What Counts as Reusable Skill Learning?}

\subsection{Source Utility and Generalization Are Distinct}

The standard-task and variant evaluations answer different parts of the learning question. Repeated positive source-task gains show that the completed artifacts can be useful in their evaluated setting. CoEvoSkills illustrates why that finding does not settle generalization: its gains recur on standard tasks while its variant averages fall below the corresponding no-skill baseline. SkillEvolver shows the broader observed reuse profile, maintaining positive averages across all three changes. Reading these results together identifies where the benefit survives, rather than treating the source score as a summary of everything the skill has learned.

SPARK illustrates a different distinction between the size and frequency of benefits. Its overall variant mean is slightly positive even though regressions outnumber improvements. A near-zero aggregate therefore does not mean that the skill has no effect: beneficial and harmful changes offset one another. The scheduling case also shows that a method with modest aggregate gains can provide a skill that succeeds across a particular family's variants. Aggregate means, outcome counts, and concrete examples describe complementary aspects of utility.

\subsection{Task Changes Probe Different Dependencies}

Representation changes test whether the executor can apply the skill when names, column arrangements, or schemas change. Instance changes require solving a new data configuration within the task family. Requirement changes test whether the skill remains useful when the requested output or constraints expand. Their diagnostic value comes from the different assumptions they disturb.

The largest separation occurs under instance changes, where SkillEvolver remains positive and the other two methods decline. The exoplanet example also shows that success under representation and requirement changes need not carry over to a replacement instance: both inspected skills fail P2. Thus the three probes should be read as a profile of applicability. Passing one change does not substitute for checking another.

\subsection{Preserve Decisions That Must Survive Reuse}

The cases suggest a concrete target for skill construction: preserve how to recognize a valid result, as well as how to produce a candidate. Signal diagnosis and schedule-repair rules remain relevant when surface inputs change. By contrast, a sequence of operations can be executable yet lead to a wrong candidate or an incomplete repair. A useful skill therefore helps the executor decide when its procedure applies, what must be adapted, and which checks must hold before accepting the output.

This diagnosis also suggests how to evaluate a completed skill. Report its paired source-task utility, examine the same frozen artifact under distinct task changes, and inspect cases where the outcomes diverge. The evaluation then describes both the benefit and the conditions under which it is expressed. In this study, the quantitative record describes those outcomes; the feedback-based account explains a pattern in the inspected skills rather than measuring an isolated feedback intervention.

\section{Related Work}

\noindent\textbf{Learning persistent skills from experience.}
SkillEvolver, SPARK, and CoEvoSkills make experience persistent through different construction loops: deployment-grounded revision, verified-trajectory distillation, and co-evolutionary verification, respectively~\cite{zhang2026skillevolver,zhou2026spark,zhang2026coevoskills}. These methods motivate our focus on the artifact delivered to a later executor. We study how that artifact's usefulness changes after its source task is varied, and interpret the resulting behavior through its retained guidance.

\noindent\textbf{Evaluating skill utility and use.}
SkillsBench evaluates skill utility across diverse tasks, while SWE-Skills-Bench studies skill use in software engineering~\cite{li2026skillsbench,han2026sweskillsbench}. SkillLearnBench evaluates continual skill generation on real-world tasks~\cite{zhong2026skilllearnbench}. From Raw Experience to Skill Consumption separates experience generation, skill extraction, and downstream consumption~\cite{huang2026rawexperience}. Our study complements these evaluations by connecting repeated source-task outcomes with controlled variations of the task and skill-level examples. The central object is the scope of usefulness of a frozen skill, rather than its score on the source task alone.

\section{Conclusion}

We examine what self-evolving agent skills preserve through repeated standard-task evaluations, task variation, and inspection of skill reuse. SkillEvolver, SPARK, and CoEvoSkills all yield positive source-task gains in both runs, but their variant results differ substantially. SkillEvolver has the broadest observed generalization profile, remaining positive under representation, instance, and requirement changes. SPARK's benefit depends on the change type, while CoEvoSkills' averages become negative. The inspected examples associate successful reuse with guidance for selecting valid results and preserving task-specific correctness rules. Together, these findings show why diagnosing skill learning requires examining how a frozen artifact guides behavior after the task changes.
